    PLE table: STRATA_PLE_CHECK=1 names the first row that decodes to an implausible value and says whether the delivered bytes are the file's (#3602)
    
    The report asks for the first data mismatch between the GGUF row bytes and what the direct reader delivered, and for an early
    check of the gathered values; neither exists. Its trace stops at "the embedding has 96 non-finite values" without saying whether
    the reader gave a wrong row or the table holds one.
    
    With STRATA_PLE_CHECK=1 (a tester switch, off by default; one static flag read per gather call when off) every direct-mode decode (read_row, collect,
    gather_batch, also from the prefetched slots) checks the row's 160 values. For a value that is not finite or beyond 1e5 (an IQ4_NL
    row cannot decode beyond 8.3e6; real PLE values are orders smaller) it re-reads that row from the file with a buffered read and prints
    one line to stderr (at most 8 per table): the row, its file offset and page offset, the value, and either "the delivered bytes ARE the
    file's bytes" or "THE DELIVERED BYTES DIFFER FROM THE FILE from byte N" with the first 8 bytes of each. That separates a reader
    fault (the bug) from a damaged table. gates/3602-ple-direct.sh sets it in its direct arms.
    
    Checked: ple_reader_test --selftest on z440 with the switch on prints the "ARE the file's bytes" line for the synthetic table's random
    rows (which decode to huge values); with it off the output and the bytes are unchanged (ple_reader_selftest passes).
    Not checked: the "DIFFER" branch has no natural trigger (that is the point); it was read, not run.

diff --git a/src/kernels/ngram.cpp b/src/kernels/ngram.cpp
